WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680
Last time, velocity was one busy enzyme's pace times how much enzyme was busy.

2
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.060
Now add an inhibitor.

3
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.780
Three kinds slow this same enzyme, yet each bends its curve a different way.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Why?

5
00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:21.523
Every enzyme is either empty or busy.

6
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:27.383
A reversible inhibitor can tie up empty enzyme, busy enzyme, or both.

7
00:00:28.763 --> 00:00:30.463
Tied-up enzyme makes no product.

8
00:00:31.843 --> 00:00:34.603
Reversible means the inhibitor can come off again.

9
00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:40.987
First, an inhibitor that grabs only empty enzyme, usually at the active site.

10
00:00:42.187 --> 00:00:45.327
Substrate and inhibitor cannot bind at the same time.

11
00:00:46.487 --> 00:00:47.947
This is competitive inhibition.

12
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.610
It now takes more substrate to make half the enzyme busy.

13
00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.810
At this dose, the half-speed point moves from 4 mM to 12.

14
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.510
That is an apparent Km.

15
00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.050
The enzyme's own Km has not changed.

16
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.450
At very high substrate, almost no empty enzyme is left to grab.

17
00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:11.970
So Vmax stays 60.

18
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:18.843
Second, an inhibitor that grabs only busy enzyme, locking it mid-reaction as ESI.

19
00:01:20.223 --> 00:01:22.163
This is uncompetitive inhibition.

20
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:28.503
By Le Chatelier's principle, pulling busy enzyme away draws more enzyme and

21
00:01:28.503 --> 00:01:29.503
substrate together.

22
00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:35.947
So half the enzyme holds substrate at a lower concentration, and the apparent Km

23
00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:40.287
falls. But much of that enzyme is stuck, making nothing.

24
00:01:41.787 --> 00:01:45.327
Vmax falls too. Here both fall threefold.

25
00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:48.017
Here is the guiding idea.

26
00:01:49.277 --> 00:01:54.397
An inhibitor's pattern is set by which enzyme it ties up: empty, busy, or both.

27
00:01:55.717 --> 00:01:59.337
Each choice moves the half-speed point and the ceiling in its own way.

28
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:03.027
Now a third inhibitor binds a separate site.

29
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:07.047
It grabs empty and busy enzyme equally well.

30
00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:10.527
At this dose, does the half-speed point move?

31
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:23.173
No. Taking the same share from empty and busy enzyme is like having less enzyme.

32
00:02:24.613 --> 00:02:26.973
Vmax falls, here from 60 to 20.

33
00:02:28.373 --> 00:02:32.833
Km stays at 4. This is pure noncompetitive inhibition.

34
00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:37.727
Pure noncompetitive is a special case of mixed inhibition.

35
00:02:39.047 --> 00:02:43.267
There, the inhibitor grips empty and busy enzyme with different strengths.

36
00:02:44.707 --> 00:02:48.487
Vmax still falls, but the apparent Km can rise or fall.

37
00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:53.570
So before reading an inhibited curve, ask what the inhibitor grabs.

38
00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.770
Grabbing empty enzyme raises the half-speed point.

39
00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:02.250
Grabbing busy enzyme lowers both values together.

40
00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.870
Grabbing both equally just lowers the ceiling.
